WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:02.200
What is hate speech?

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.580
And should the government ban it?

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.740
We asked college students at the University
of Southern California.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.680
If somebody uses a racial slur?

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.260
That's hate speech? Yeah.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.640
I think that would be a reason to, like a
crime.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.600
What if somebody says 'Muslims shouldn't be
allowed in the US.'

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:22.200
Yeah, that's hate speech.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.520
What if somebody denies the Holocaust?

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.240
I, no, I don't have any understanding for
that.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.879
You shouldn't be able to blatantly lie about
something, especially like the Holocaust.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.089
A female comedian says 'men are scum?'

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.960
I don't believe that's hate speech.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.760
'All white people are racist?'

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.740
No, I'm gonna say no.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.500
I don't know.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.380
The First Amendment grants absolute protection
of even the most vile speech

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.030
so long as it doesn't directly incite violence.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.240
I hate white people, all of them.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.900
Every last iota of a cracker, I hate him.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.690
But is the tide turning in America?

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:09.320
40 percent of Americans now think that the
government should regulate so-called hate speech.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.700
But it turns out that defining hate speech
is easier said than done.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.540
Everyone thinks, this is hate speech, this
is hate speech.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.280
Many who advocate hate speech laws in 2018

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.340
seem motivated by the rise of Trumpism-

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.410
'build that wall'

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.060
-and hateful rhetoric on the right.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.080
And college campuses have become the epicenter
of the free speech debate

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.200
with incidents of college students shouting
down

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.960
and even physically attacking controversial
speakers

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.200
becoming an increasingly common occurrence.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.880
So we talked to students at the University
of Southern California

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.900
about free speech and hate speech.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.720
One common refrain on campus is that hateful
speech is actually equivalent to violence.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.260
Do you think hate speech by itself is an act
of violence?

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:51.720
I do.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:52.520
Yes.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:54.160
No.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.520
Your words can't, like, physically harm someone.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.200
I think violence is more than physical, it's
emotional, psychological.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.980
If hate speech is violence, maybe it makes
sense to regulate it

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.460
like Canada, Australia and most of Europe
do.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.100
Many of the students we spoke to seemed open
to the idea.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.560
I think if people can understand the power
of words

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.400
then maybe hate speech as violence could help
us have restrictions

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.540
that are not damaging or that could stop at
a certain place.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.560
Those kind of opinions are better said, left
unsaid

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.400
because it just incites violence and hatred
among everyone, you know.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.720
Are you in favor of regulations that would
stop people from doing that

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.380
or would make that a crime?

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.060
Yeah.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.480
I would have no problem if it is regulated.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.240
But then who determines what constitutes hate
speech?

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.620
While most respondents to a recent poll agreed
that hate speech is immoral,

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.040
82% said it would be difficult to ban because
people can't agree on what is hateful.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.580
Could people form a consensus around whether
these things are hate speech?

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:50.580
Definitely not.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.380
I think it would be really hard to agree.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.600
And so does that give you pause

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.520
and make you think maybe the regulation would
not do what I would hope it would do?

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.180
Yeah.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.200
Like obviously that would be ideal if something
like that could be into place

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:08.150
I just don't really see how it would be.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.260
So for, I'll give you an example,

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.930
so there was a french comedian and he was
jailed for an anti-Semitic joke

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.560
would you support that type of action?

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.000
No.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.560
Advocates of hate speech laws often say

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.500
that racists cloak themselves in the veil
of free speech to attack vulnerable populations.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.920
Having free speech is a great right for everyone,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.520
but when the more powerful have it, I mean,
it's going to obviously work to their advantage

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.319
more than the less powerful.

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:37.299
But social media provides examples of how
speech codes often backfire

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:39.980
and hurt the very minorities they're intended
to protect.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.940
Facebook and Twitter are private companies

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:44.420
free to ban whatever they deem to be hate speech.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.160
But their attempts to do so have resulted
in bans of feminists

00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:49.120
saying that 'all white men are trash.'

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.939
and the platform suspended rapper Lil B for
posting that

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:56.220
'white people are the only ones who really
love they guns you can tell they are violent people.'

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.300
Hateful speech might be hurtful, stupid or
just plain wrong.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.800
But if we deprive ourselves of the opportunity
to hear it

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.160
we also lose the opportunity to rebut it.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.740
And the person who's willing to speak forbidden
words gains a special power.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:11.500
When Austria imprisoned historian David Irving
for Holocaust denial,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:15.180
he was greeted with a hero's welcome by a
crowd of ten thousand in Budapest

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.840
shortly after his release.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.880
The government had turned him into a martyr.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.840
In America attempts to shut down or shout
down speakers

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.900
have almost certainly backfired as well,

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.600
by boosting the visibility of provocateurs
like Milo Yiannopoulos.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.900
They're bringing drugs, they're bringing crime,
they're rapists,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.460
and some I assume are good people.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.480
Trump saying any of these things should definitely
be regulated

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.700
because he has so much influence and him saying
these things in speeches and stuff.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:46.900
Trump saying things about minorities like
Hispanics and women and Muslims.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.860
Does it concern you that if the government
is the one that is regulating these things

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.740
that people like Trump would be the one deciding
what is protected speech and what is not?

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.120
Yeah, of course that's another concern.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.420
Granting the government the power to decide
what's acceptable to say

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.560
presumes that what brings us closer to the
truth is not the free exchange of ideas

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.960
but the wise decision making of politicians.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.100
And many of the college students we spoke
to agreed

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:12.980
that being exposed to challenging and even
threatening ideas

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.370
is an important aspect of the intellectual
journey.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.050
And what do you think about people shutting down
speakers on campuses?

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:24.400
I don't like that at all, honestly I follow
a lot of what goes on at Berkely

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.840
where they, like, protesting not having these
far right speakers come.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.520
I think that's dangerous, to be perfectly
honest.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.100
Here at USC and and on college campuses

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.900
it's important that we hear everything that
we possibly can

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.960
whether that be something that we don't like
or something that rubs us the wrong way,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.960
I think it's important that we hear it because
we're going to go out into the world

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.320
and we're going to hear it there and it's
important that we're able to hear it here

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.880
and form our own opinions, our own ideas.

